+### Exit codes
+
+One table, in one place: `EXIT_CODE_TABLE` in `src/lead_desk/constants.py`, one row per program, code and meaning; the table below is printed from it by `tools/render_exit_table.py` (a test fails when the two differ), so no other page repeats a program's exit numbers. A number can mean different things in different programs: several of the small numbers (the pinned list `SAME_NUMBER_DIFFERENT_MEANING`), so read a number together with the program that returned it. `server` is `python -m lead_desk.server` (the desk); `crash_desk`, `feed`, `reset_check` and `run_real_check` are the Python scripts; the other four are the shell and PowerShell scripts. Every program that reads the settings itself gives the same code (`EXIT_SCRIPT_SETTING_REFUSED`) for a refused setting, except the desk (its own refusal) and `crash_desk`/`start_all` (they pass on the desk's own refusal of a late setting). Two wait settings have their own code: a bad `START_ALL_WAIT_SECONDS` ends `start_all` with its own "start did not complete" row, and a bad `RESET_STOP_WAIT_SECONDS` ends `reset` with its own "refused" row. `start_all` passes on only the three codes of the desk that its rows name; every other end of the desk (including a desk that stopped with 0) is its own "start did not complete" row. The simulated-kill code (`EXIT_SIMULATED_KILL`) is only the crash script stopping the desk on purpose.
